  "content": {
    "title": "Why Tennessee basketball vs Miami (Ohio) in March Madness reminds Vols of SEC rival",
    "text": "Updated March 19, 2026, 5:18 p.m. ETTennessee basketball is preparing for its March Madness game against Miami (Ohio) by comparing them to SEC foe Alabama.Miami (Ohio) has the second-best offense in the country, averaging 90.7 points per game, similar to Alabama's top-ranked offense.The RedHawks are one of the nation's best 3-point shooting teams, making 39% of their attempts.Tennessee's defense, which held Alabama to its two lowest-scoring games, plans to leverage its size and physicality against the RedHawks.PHILADELPHIA - As Tennessee basketball watched Miami (Ohio) sink 16 3-pointers in its 89-79 First Four win over SMU, a familiar foe came to mind.Tennessee expects an offensive attack similar to Alabama when the No. 6 seed Vols (22-11) take on the No. 11 RedHawks (32-1) in the first round of March Madness at Xfinity Mobile Arena on March 20 (4:25 ET, TBS).\"Alabama's just a ton of 3's, quick offense,\" Tennessee forward J.P. Estrella said March 19. \"We're thinking about (Miami of Ohio) the same way. I feel like we're kind of preparing a little bit like that Alabama game.\"Buy Tennessee basketball NCAA tournament ticketsDespite the strength of schedule disparity between the SEC and MAC, the stats support the comparison. Miami (Ohio) has the second-best offense in the country. Its 90.7 points per game trail only Alabama's 91.7.The RedHawks don't shoot as many 3-pointers as the Tide, who lead the country with 35.3 attempts a game. However, Miami (Ohio) shoots a scorching 39% from 3, good for No. 8 in the nation.Both Miami (Ohio) and Alabama average 16.1 assists, but Tennessee associate head coach Justin Gainey believes the RedHawks use their passing prowess in a different way.\"From a pace standpoint, from an ability to score the ball at a high clip and shooting ability,\" Gainey said about similarities between the opponents. \"I'd say (Miami of Ohio) probably does it more with the pass. I think they do a really good job. They get paint touches, but they can really pass the ball.\"The Vols are used to the shoot-at-will approach that Miami (Ohio) employed against SMU. Tennessee split its season series against Alabama, but it held the Tide to its two lowest-scoring outings of the season. The Vols' SEC-best defense limited Alabama to 73 and 71 points in two matchups, well below the Tide's season average.Tennessee also likes its matchup on the interior. Forward Jaylen Carey expects the frontcourt's physicality to overwhelm the RedHawks on both ends.\"Oh yeah, we're much bigger and stronger,\" Carey said. \"We're going out there and punking them early, letting them know early we're going to impose our will, but that's something that you have to do and maintain it for 40 minutes.\"I don't think they've ever seen a physical team like us.\"Wynton Jackson covers high school sports for Knox News. Email: wynton.jackson@knoxnews.comSupport strong local journalism and unlock premium perks: knoxnews.com/subscribe",
